  "tarball_url": "https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/tarball/v1.22.0",
  "zipball_url": "https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/zipball/v1.22.0",
  "body": "`mbx reserve` lets a VM, container, or other non-mbx command hold part of the shared compiler pool while it runs. `mbx explain --last` now names the setting to change when a miss comes from a checkout difference. This release also fixes several build-script caching bugs, stops idle containers from waiting on another container's memory pressure, and fixes `DYLD_*` variables being stripped by the signed macOS binary.\n\n## Added\n\n- **`mbx reserve` holds scheduler capacity for an external command** ([#643](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/pull/643), @jdx). Work that mbx doesn't run, such as a VM or container build, can now wait for and hold a share of the machine-wide compiler pool, so it doesn't compete with mbx builds:\n\n  ```sh\n  mbx reserve --memory 6GiB --cpus 4 -- ./run-windows-build-in-vm.sh\n  ```\n\n  - `--memory` counts against `scheduler.memory` and requires a memory budget. `--priority low` gives way to other work when the reservation is waiting to start. Once a low-priority reservation is admitted, it keeps its capacity. The default priority is normal.\n  - A separate worker process holds the reservation until the command exits, even if the `mbx reserve` process is killed. The command's exit status is passed through.\n  - mbx commands run inside the reserved command skip scheduler admission, because the reservation already covers them.\n  - A `+toolchain` prefix on the wrapped command is rejected.\n\n- **`mbx explain --last` names the setting behind a cross-checkout miss** ([#639](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/pull/639), @jdx). If the miss is compared against another checkout's recording, the diff is now followed by `hint:` lines:\n  - `CARGO_MANIFEST_DIR` differs: the crate is keyed to its checkout. `MBX_SHARE_WORKSPACE_ROOT=1` still lets its dependents share.\n  - `OUT_DIR` differs: check that `MBX_SHARE_OUT_DIR` isn't set to 0. It is on by default.\n  - `--remap-path-prefix` differs: set `MBX_SHARE_WORKSPACE_ROOT` to the same value in both checkouts.\n  - Another environment variable differs: set it to the same value in both checkouts, or unset it.\n  - Only an upstream artifact differs: if that crate is a workspace member, `MBX_SHARE_WORKSPACE_ROOT=1` makes both checkouts produce the same output.\n\n  Real flag or profile differences, such as `--opt-level`, get no hint. When a baseline exists from the same workspace, mbx still compares against it first.\n\n## Fixed\n\n### Build-script caching\n\n- **Build scripts that write into their own declared inputs are no longer cached** ([#640](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/pull/640), @jdx). If a script changed or deleted a path it lists in `cargo:rerun-if-changed`, its result could be stored under the key for the tree it left behind. Later builds could then restore output that a real run wouldn't produce. Now, if any declared input changes during the run, mbx doesn't record the result and reports the bypass as `build-script-wrote-its-inputs`. Cargo reruns the script on the next build, and that run is cached normally.\n- **Build scripts that link from the target directory outside `OUT_DIR` rerun instead of being replayed** ([#635](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/pull/635), @jdx, fixes [#633](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/issues/633)). A cache hit restores only `OUT_DIR`. A script like `v8`'s, which puts `librusty_v8.a` under `target/<profile>/gn_out/obj` and adds that path with `rustc-link-search`, failed in a second checkout with `could not find native static library`. This also applies to `rustc-link-arg`, `rustc-cdylib-link-arg`, and `rustc-flags`. mbx no longer caches these scripts, and it ignores existing cache entries that contain such directives. Link paths inside `OUT_DIR` or outside the target directory, such as system libraries, are still cached.\n- **Restored build scripts stay in CI cache exports** ([#629](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/pull/629), @jdx). Before, `mbx cache export --group` dropped build scripts that had been restored from the cache. Unchanged CI jobs then switched back and forth between a full restore and rerunning build scripts, along with the C compilations they start. Restored scripts are now recorded in the build, so every export keeps them. If mbx can't record a restored script, it warns and uses the cached result instead of rerunning the script.\n- **Target seeding skips build-script units whose output files name the donor checkout** ([#641](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/pull/641), @jdx). Seeding already skipped a unit if the script's stdout mentioned the donor's path. It didn't check the files under `out/`, so a unit whose `CMakeCache.txt` or a similar file recorded the donor's path was still copied, and the new checkout's build broke. Seeding now also scans those files, and checks donor paths written with either slash direction. A unit that matches, can't be read, or has too much output to scan is left for its script to run normally.\n\n### Scheduler\n\n- **Containers that share a cache directory track memory pressure separately** ([#634](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/pull/634), @jbellis). Before, all containers using one cache wrote to a single shared pressure state. Their different cgroup counters looked like large memory stalls, so idle containers could hold compilations for many minutes. One C compilation waited 22 minutes and then ran in 0.13 s. Each memory cgroup now has its own state under `<cache>/scheduler/pressure/`, so pressure in one container pauses only that container's new compilations. Permits and leases are still shared across the pool. A container under real pressure still waits for leases held in other containers. If compiler supervision suspends a compilation in any container, new compilations still wait in all of them.\n\n### macOS\n\n- **The signed macOS release binary keeps `DYLD_*` environment variables** ([#630](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/pull/630), @jdx). The hardened runtime removed `DYLD_*` variables from the environment, so tools mbx started could lose the library path Cargo set for them. Release builds then printed `stripping debug info with rust-objcopy failed: signal: 6 (SIGABRT)` and `dyld: Library not loaded: @rpath/libLLVM.dylib`. The binary is now signed with an entitlement that keeps these variables. Cached results from affected builds still contain the old warning, so run `mbx cache remove` once after upgrading if you saw it.\n\n### Stats\n\n- **`mbx stats` is faster on machines with many checkouts** ([#631](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/pull/631), @jdx). Sizing managed targets, scanning the store, and estimating sharing now run at the same time on several threads. On one store with 131 workspaces and about 212k objects, the time dropped from about 28 s to about 6.5 s. The output is unchanged.\n- **\"Copying avoided\" is no longer called \"reflinked\"** ([#638](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/pull/638), @jdx). This number also counts outputs restored by hard link, so `mbx stats` and the TUI Store screen now just call it \"copying avoided\". The value itself is unchanged.\n\n### Documentation\n\n- **Guides and CLI help match current behavior** ([#637](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/pull/637), @jdx). Examples:\n  - The cache server install now uses `cargo install --locked --git https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington-cache mbx-cache`. The `mbx-cache` crate on crates.io is a placeholder.\n  - The GitHub Action parallel-steps example now sets `github-cache-mode: objects`, so the store is saved.\n  - The docs now say that on Unix, mbx hard links store objects when it can't clone them.\n  - The short help for each command in `mbx --help` is now one sentence, with caveats moved to the long help.\n\n## Upgrade notes\n\n- Build-script cache entries miss once after upgrading, because the build-script cache key changed (#640).\n\n**Full Changelog**: https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/compare/v1.21.1...v1.22.0",
